# PART 9 — TOOL DISPATCH, AUTHORIZATION, AND FAILURE SAFETY

The LLM interprets the user. Trusted backend code decides which operation is permitted and executes it.

Use the existing capability registry and actual diagnostic modules. Do not permit the model to execute arbitrary commands or invent a capability ID.

Before any tool runs, validate:

- Intended operation.
- Required target and parameters.
- Whether the target was explicit or contextually resolved.
- User's execution constraints.
- Authorization and current RBAC.
- Existing SSRF protection and URL/IP validation.
- Timeouts and cancellation.
- The capability's actual supported scope.

A bare topic is not sufficient authorization to launch a workflow.

A vague problem description should not launch every available diagnostic. Ask the smallest useful clarification unless context resolves the problem.

Explicit read-only requests can execute under existing permissions and policy.

The model must respect instructions such as:

- `Don't run anything yet.`
- `Just explain.`
- `Explain what the previous result means without making new checks.`

State-changing operations must remain subject to existing safeguards and confirmation requirements.

When the AI provider is unavailable:

- Do not claim live semantic interpretation succeeded.
- Do not automatically launch a broad diagnostic.
- Do not invent a target.
- Use an established safe fast path only when it genuinely determines the requested operation.
- Otherwise ask for clarification or honestly report that semantic interpretation is currently unavailable.

Maintain session ownership, cross-user isolation, bounded memory, history provenance, cancellation safety, and provider privacy policies.

